version 4
# Source: published: E. M. Izhikevich, "Which model to use for cortical spiking neurons?", IEEE Trans. Neural Networks 15(5) (2004) 1063-1070, doi:10.1109/TNN.2004.832719 (Fig. 1 panels G, H, K, L, the examples/ode/izh_2004* models; checked at Crossref, W537).
# The smooth part of Izhikevich's simple model, the rest state of its panels (W416, #478):
#   v' = 0.04 v^2 + k1 v + k0 - u + Ib,  u' = a (b v - u).
# The reset (v = 30: v = c, u = u + d) is no part of it: a continuation of the rest state, as the base current Ib
# varies, shows the bifurcation that gives a panel its name: a fold (class 1 excitable, integrator) or a Hopf point
# (class 2 excitable, resonator). tests/auto's izh_* descriptions set the parameters of the panel (the models'
# examples/ode/izh_2004*: G, L, H, K) and the rest state it starts from.
par a = 0.02, b = -0.1, k1 = 4.1, k0 = 108, Ib = 0
init v = -60, u = 6
v' = 0.04*v^2+k1*v+k0-u+Ib
u' = a*(b*v-u)
